{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And Avimelekh said, etc., וְגַם אָנֹכִי, etc.</b> One needs to know why he went back to say ״וְגַם אָנֹכִי.״ And it appears in a further fine point, his saying <b>לֹא יָדַעְתִּי מִי עָשָׂה</b>, etc. — why was it not sufficient to say ״לֹא יָדַעְתִי,״ the matter being self-evident that it refers back to Avraham's words?" "<b>Rather,</b> the intention is that he knew they had stolen from Avraham the well of water, but he did not know who exactly the thieves were. And accordingly, Avraham would say that it was upon him to investigate who the wrongdoers were; to this he replied that Avraham too acted improperly by not informing the king, and he was waiting and expecting Avraham to come, and then he would have rendered judgment for him. And since Avraham did not regard him as significant, he too did not know — and this is his saying <b>וְגַם אָנֹכִי לֹא שָׁמַעְתִּי</b>, etc. — meaning: I did not seek to know, and I remained stripped of knowledge until the day you informed me."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:42.192025531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}